## 5. Roles, Permissions, and Authentication

### Roles

- `author`
- `admin`

Every protected backend endpoint must derive the identity and role from verified server-side authentication. Do not trust a user-supplied `role`, `authorId`, or `ownerId` in request bodies.

### Authentication behavior

- Email/password login. No OTP or social login is required.
- Normalize emails consistently (trim and lowercase).
- Hash passwords with bcrypt using a suitable work factor. Never store plaintext passwords.
- Use a signed JWT in a `HttpOnly`, `Secure` cookie in production, with an appropriate `SameSite` setting.
- Configure CORS with the exact allowed frontend origin(s) and credentials enabled. Do not use wildcard origins with credentials.
- Use a CSRF mitigation appropriate to cookie authentication. At minimum, validate Origin/Referer for state-changing browser requests and use a CSRF token if needed by the deployment setup.
- Implement logout by clearing the cookie with the same cookie options used to set it.
- Use a reasonable token expiry and handle expired/invalid tokens cleanly.
- Add login rate limiting and generic login failure messages to reduce account enumeration.
- Validate all environment variables on startup. Fail fast with a clear developer-facing message if required secrets are missing; do not print secret values.
- Do not include password hashes or tokens in API responses.

### Authorization matrix

