## 9. Data Model

Use Mongoose schemas with timestamps and explicit validation. The final schema may adapt to the supplied JSON, but must preserve its records and support the requirements below.

### 9.1 User

Suggested fields:

```text
User
- _id
- name
- email (normalized, unique index)
- passwordHash
- role: author | admin
- createdAt
- updatedAt
- isActive
```

If the sample JSON uses a different author field structure, write an explicit, tested import mapping instead of dropping data.

### 9.2 Book

Suggested fields:

```text
Book
- _id
- sourceId (optional original dataset ID, unique where appropriate)
- authorId -> User/Author
- title
- isbn (optional for unpublished/unfinished books)
- genre
- publicationDate (nullable)
- status / productionStage
- mrp (nullable number)
- currency
- totalCopiesSold (nullable number)
- totalRoyaltyEarned (nullable number)
- royaltyPaid (nullable number)
- royaltyPending (nullable number)
- distributionDetails (optional, based on source dataset)
- createdAt
- updatedAt
```

Important:
- Avoid using `0` as a substitute for unknown values.
- Use `null` for unknown/not applicable fields when supported.
- Store money as a numeric decimal-safe representation where practical (integer minor units or Decimal128). If the dataset uses ordinary numbers, map consistently and avoid floating-point arithmetic for newly calculated money.
- Keep the source data's actual field values and meanings. Do not recalculate the sample's royalties unless explicitly required.

### 9.3 Ticket

Suggested fields:

```text
Ticket
- _id
- ticketNumber (human-friendly unique reference, optional but useful)
- authorId -> User
- bookId -> Book | null
- subject
- description
- status: open | in_progress | resolved | closed
- category: one of the six required categories
- aiCategoryRecommendation
- categorySource: ai | admin | default
- priority: critical | high | medium | low
- aiPriorityRecommendation
- prioritySource: ai | admin | default
- aiState: pending | completed | failed | not_requested
- aiModel
- aiPromptVersion
- aiLastErrorCode (safe internal code; no secret/provider payload)
- assigneeId -> User/admin | null
- lastPublicResponseAt (optional)
- createdAt
- updatedAt
```

Do not rely only on free-form status/category/priority strings. Enforce enums in both request validation and schema.

### 9.4 TicketMessage

Recommended unified message collection:

```text
TicketMessage
- _id
- ticketId -> Ticket
- senderId -> User
- senderRole: author | admin
- visibility: public | internal
- body
- createdAt
- updatedAt
```

Only admins can create `internal` messages. Authors can create an initial ticket description; follow assignment scope and do not add author follow-up messaging unless implemented deliberately. Public admin replies are `visibility=public`; internal notes are `visibility=internal`.

Alternative: keep original query in Ticket and only store replies/notes in TicketMessage. If using that approach, maintain the same authorization boundaries.

### 9.5 TicketAuditEvent (recommended)

```text
TicketAuditEvent
- _id
- ticketId
- actorId
- action: created | status_changed | category_changed | priority_changed | assigned | response_sent | internal_note_added | ai_generated
- previousValue (optional, safe)
- newValue (optional, safe)
- createdAt
```

Use this for traceability, especially when admins override AI suggestions or change status. Never expose private audit payloads indiscriminately to authors.

### 9.6 Indexes

Create indexes for:
- User email (unique).
- Book authorId.
- Ticket authorId + createdAt.
- Ticket status + priority + createdAt / updatedAt as appropriate.
