## 13. Real-Time Implementation

Use SSE for a simple, suitable near-real-time implementation.

- Authenticated client opens an SSE stream for a ticket or admin queue.
- Server authorizes the connection before sending events.
- Events should contain only identifiers and safe metadata, or a carefully filtered payload. Avoid sending full ticket data to all subscribers.
- Event types can include `ticket.updated`, `ticket.response.created`, `ticket.status.changed`, `ticket.assignment.changed`, and admin-only `ticket.internal_note.created`.
- **Internal-note events are admin-only.** Never broadcast them to an author stream.
- Enforce ticket ownership before an author subscribes.
- Use heartbeat events and close streams when clients disconnect.
- Since SSE connections may be interrupted, clients must refetch current state on reconnect.
- If cross-instance pub/sub is not implemented, document that an in-memory event broker only works reliably for a single backend instance. For this assignment, a single Render instance is acceptable; for horizontal scale, add Redis pub/sub or a durable event mechanism.
- Add a small polling fallback if SSE is unavailable or fails repeatedly.

